 @chengcao418  In a real usage scenario you're more likely to cap the FPS and invest your GPU usage in rendering features or resolution (at least if you're a bit smart about tuning your game experience). For benchmarking it makes sense to normalize one thing (say, fps) and compare another parameter (say, power consumption). Especially if both of the things you're testing against are sensitive to each other.

In real life the entire discussion about "performance per frame" is pointless, though. Ultimately you need a PSU to serve the GPU at its maximum power draw and the energy cost of the part is gonna be your average total power draw. Per-frame efficiency is irrelevant, the point is total power draw per unit of time at that point.

Honestly, what I care about is whether you need to upgrade your PSU if you had a 30 series card plugged into a decent 850W part that is also feeding the kind of high end CPU you need to match with the 4090, and reviewers don't seem to have an agreement on that one at this point.

